="316" name="Google Shape;316;p43"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="457200" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="8229600" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="A diagram shows the hydrolysis reaction of Acetyl-CoA to form acetate.&#xA;The reaction begins at the top with acetyl-CoA, labeled right. CH3 bonds right to C, which double bonds up and right to O and down and right to S-CoA, shown in pink. Below is a down arrow labeled &quot;hydrolysis;&quot; on the left is a convergent arrow showing the addition of H2O and on the right is a divergent arrow showing the loss of CoASH, shown in pink with the H in black. Below is the intermediate, acetic acid, labeled right. The CH3 bonds right to C, which double bonds up and right to O and down and right to OH. The C, O, and OH are all shaded orange. Below is an equilibrium arrow labeled &quot;ionization,&quot; with a divergent arrow showing the loss of H+. Below is the acetate, labeled right, which consists of the CH3 bonded right to C, which bonds up and right and down and right to O. Between the two O atoms is a curved, pink dashed line. Each O has a pink negative delta sign next to it. This region is shaded orange and is labeled &quot;resonance stabilization&quot; below. At the bottom is a gray box containing the chemical equation and the free energy. The equation reads: Acetyl-CoA [plus] H2O yields acetate- (minus charge) [plus] CoA [plus] H+. Below, the free energy (delta G prime) is -31.4 kJ/mol." id="317" name="Google Shape;317;p43"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><